WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.578
WasmGPU is a WebGPU and WebAssembly engine for

00:00:04.678 --> 00:00:09.012
scientific rendering and computing in the browser.

00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:12.042
This overview explains how it is built,

00:00:12.142 --> 00:00:14.752
and how its documentation is organized.

00:00:16.666 --> 00:00:21.772
WasmGPU is written in three languages, each with a distinct job.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.974
TypeScript implements the public API and the WebGPU

00:00:26.074 --> 00:00:30.761
engine, including the renderer, compute, and interaction tools.

00:00:31.166 --> 00:00:34.667
Rust implements the WebAssembly driver, for memory,

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:37.914
transforms, math, culling, and asset decoding.

00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:42.329
WGSL shaders do the rendering and compute work on

00:00:42.429 --> 00:00:46.425
the GPU, and are bundled into JavaScript as text.

00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:51.540
The architecture is organized into five layers.

00:00:51.966 --> 00:00:57.200
The public API is the WasmGPU class and its factory surface.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.331
The WebGPU engine runs the frame loop, the renderer,

00:01:01.431 --> 00:01:05.457
and the interaction tools, beside the compute subsystem.

00:01:05.866 --> 00:01:09.075
The object and data model holds scenes,

00:01:09.175 --> 00:01:12.977
transforms, objects, glTF assets, and interop.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.737
The WebAssembly driver provides heap and frame-arena memory, and

00:01:17.837 --> 00:01:21.620
Rust routines for transforms, math, bounds, and culling.

00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:25.025
Browser resources are the GPU device and

00:01:25.125 --> 00:01:28.504
queue, caches, buffers, textures, and passes.

00:01:28.933 --> 00:01:31.494
In the diagram below this video, solid

00:01:31.594 --> 00:01:35.276
arrows show creation, ownership, references, or calls.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.547
Dashed arrows show data moving through

00:01:38.647 --> 00:01:41.573
WebAssembly memory or WebGPU resources.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.410
An application starts with WasmGPU.create, given

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:50.418
a canvas and an optional descriptor.

00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:55.341
It initializes WebAssembly, creates the WebGPU renderer, and

00:01:55.441 --> 00:01:59.949
builds the instance's compute, Python, and scaling services.

00:02:00.366 --> 00:02:02.976
Then run starts a browser animation loop.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:07.424
Each frame resets the frame arena, measures the time step, and

00:02:07.524 --> 00:02:11.748
calls your callback, which usually renders a scene with a camera.

00:02:12.166 --> 00:02:16.498
Render updates transforms, builds draw lists, encodes the GPU

00:02:16.598 --> 00:02:19.768
passes, and submits them to the device queue.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.713
Data moves between JavaScript, WebAssembly, and the

00:02:25.813 --> 00:02:28.213
GPU along a few explicit paths.

00:02:28.633 --> 00:02:33.095
Typed arrays are copied into WebAssembly memory, where Rust processes

00:02:33.195 --> 00:02:36.533
them, and results are read back through typed views.

00:02:36.933 --> 00:02:40.688
Queue writes copy those views, or plain typed arrays,

00:02:40.788 --> 00:02:44.470
into GPU buffers that render and compute passes use.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.878
To read results back, the GPU copies a buffer range into a

00:02:48.978 --> 00:02:54.293
staging buffer, which is mapped asynchronously and returned as a typed array.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.866
For rendering, meshes combine geometry and materials,

00:02:59.966 --> 00:03:03.561
and five scientific primitives cover points, glyphs,

00:03:03.661 --> 00:03:06.617
graphs, Gaussian splats, and regular cells.

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:11.844
For computing, you write WGSL pipelines or call built-in

00:03:11.944 --> 00:03:17.194
kernels, with CPU and GPU ndarrays and asynchronous readback.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.039
Interop connects the engine to Python through Pyodide, to

00:03:22.139 --> 00:03:27.057
foreign WebAssembly modules, and to declarative WebGPU layouts.

00:03:28.966 --> 00:03:32.244
The documentation has eight sections, and each opens

00:03:32.344 --> 00:03:34.648
with an overview video like this one.

00:03:35.066 --> 00:03:38.619
Architecture shows how the subsystems fit together.

00:03:38.719 --> 00:03:42.774
Compute covers pipelines, ndarrays, kernels, and readback.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.134
Interaction covers controls, picking, annotations, and scaling.

00:03:48.234 --> 00:03:52.849
Interoperability covers memory, Python, and WebGPU layouts.

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:58.326
Mathematics covers vectors, matrices, and quaternions. Objects

00:03:58.426 --> 00:04:02.404
covers geometry, materials, primitives, and glTF.

00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:06.993
Render covers the lifecycle, culling, effects, and transforms.

00:04:07.093 --> 00:04:10.498
World covers scenes, cameras, lights, and overlays.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.428
WasmGPU is installed from npm, or loaded

00:04:16.528 --> 00:04:19.937
as a script from the jsDelivr CDN.

00:04:20.366 --> 00:04:24.382
A minimal program creates the engine, a scene, a camera,

00:04:24.482 --> 00:04:27.690
a light, and a mesh, then renders inside run.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:32.672
The examples gallery shows complete programs built with these APIs.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.864
In short: three languages, five layers, a frame

00:04:37.964 --> 00:04:42.158
loop, explicit data paths, and eight documentation sections.

00:04:43.566 --> 00:04:46.179
Continue with the architecture guide, or

00:04:46.279 --> 00:04:48.757
open any section of the documentation.
